ates paperwork. So 2.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal addresses meeting logging mandate by automating documentation. That is directly related. Do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes. So 4 = solves an identified problem directly related.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability? Will automatic pipeline solve logging mandate? It helps with technical documentation but mandate requires logs recording events, not just documentation. It bridges gap between raw logs and Recital 71 requirements. It may contribute but not fully solve logging mandate (still need logs). It removes administrative burden. It's partial solution. So effectiveness =3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial. Might be 4? It doesn't ensure logs are recorded automatically; assumes logs exist. The mandate is automatic recording of events. This pipeline converts logs to docs. So it helps compliance but not core logging. Probably contributes. So 3.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented. Scripts pull data from experiment tracking to populate templates automatically during build. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. That's 3? Easy to implement at low cost =4. Some changes or reasonably feasible =3. Could be easy? Needs integration. Probably some changes. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. Automated documentation is common, acceptable. 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order listed, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id. Likely list. Could be JSON object with fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2044\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 2\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sentences concise.\n\nLet's craft.\n\noriginality_evidence: The automated log-to-documentation pipeline is a familiar DevOps reporting pattern applied to AI compliance, not ingenious or surprising. -> score 2\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces tooling to automate paperwork but keeps compliance as separate logging practice without changing the lab's R&D-compliance relationship. -> score 2\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses the mandate to produce up-to-date technical documentation from automatic logs required by Article 12 and Recital 71. -> score 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Automating documentation reduces manual burden and improves traceability, but it assumes logs already exist and does not itself create the mandated automatic event logging. -> score 3\n\nimplementability_evidence: Building scripts to pull experiment tracking metadata into compliance templates during build is reasonably feasible with existing CI tooling. -> score 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Automating compliance paperwork is a common, norm-conforming practice that violates no legal or social sensibilities. -> score 4\n\nReturn.\n\n",
