        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\nWe need rate proposal P1210.\n\nProposal: High-Risk Project Compliance Audit. Comprehensive technical review... map existing data flows against Article 12 requirements and document every missing telemetry point... prevents expensive disruptive retrospective redesigns...\n\nWe need six subscales in order: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide evidence sentence then score.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is audit to identify gaps. Does it solve problem? It will contribute to solution but is only partial solution; audit identifies deficits but doesn't implement logging. So effectiveness = 3? Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 4? \"reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\" Audit alone does not solve logging mandate, just maps gaps. So 3.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related: do audit to get gap map, which is part of meeting logging mandate. Seems direct. Score 4? solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented. Comprehensive technical review by senior engineers map data flows... Some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Likely 3? Could be easy? Requires senior engineer time. Probably some changes reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms. Audit is common. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nAudit is standard compliance practice. Probably paradigm preserving = 1. Could be slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces mapping process but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2? \"introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\". Audit is standard. I think 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. Hmm audit does change way of working temporarily. Probably paradigm preserving 1.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nCommon, mundane, boring =1. Audit is common. Score 1.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating before number.\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nFormat likely JSON? Probably just lines. Safer to output structured.\n\nProvide:\n\nid: P1210\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nLet's produce.\n\nEnsure evidence one sentence.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Audit maps missing telemetry but does not itself implement logging capabilities required by Article 12.\n\nApplicability evidence: Identifying gaps directly addresses the lab's need to meet Article 12 logging mandate.\n\nImplementability evidence: Mapping data flows requires senior engineer time but uses existing staff and documentation.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Internal compliance audits are standard business practice and raise no legal or normative concerns.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal treats compliance as a conventional gap analysis without altering how R&D relates to regulation.\n\nOriginality evidence: Compliance audits for regulatory requirements are common, mundane responses in AI labs.\n\nScores as above.\n\nCheck ordering: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nReturn.\n\n",
