        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rating for proposal P2791.\n\nWe must give evidence sentence then score.\n\nSubscales in order: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nLet's interpret proposal: Automated Provenance and Logging Framework. standardized software layer automatically captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, runtime events... custom middleware API integrates deeply with existing training pipelines to log critical telemetry into immutable ledger. ensures compliance without manually burdening researchers.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDoes automated provenance framework solve logging mandate? It captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, runtime events, logs telemetry into immutable ledger. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events (logs) over lifetime, recording period of each use, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons... Also recital mentions technical documentation containing general characteristics, capabilities, limitations, algorithms, data, training, testing validation processes etc.\n\nProposal captures hyperparameters, dataset versions, runtime events. Might be partial? It addresses automatic logging, reduces manual burden. Seems reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Could it solve main problem? Possibly not fully covers Article 12 3 specifics (period of each use, reference database...). But maybe contributes.\n\nAnchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve the main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but only partial)\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nI think it's reasonable and will contribute but is it full solution? It mentions logging critical telemetry data into immutable ledger. Could be sufficient. Might be 4? Hmm \"absolute traceability\". Might claim solve. I'd say 4? Let's see: requirement is automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, enabling recording events relevant for risk... For high-risk systems point 1(a) Annex III minimum logging. Proposal mentions hyperparameters dataset versions runtime events. Does not explicitly mention period of each use start/end, reference database, input data for match, identification of natural persons. Might be incomplete.\n\nProbably effectiveness = 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Because it doesn't guarantee compliance with specific Article 12(3) logging items.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 = significant change expensive difficult but not totally impossible\nProposal: develop custom middleware API integrates deeply with existing training pipelines to log into immutable ledger. That's significant change, custom development. Probably 2? Or 3? \"develop a custom middleware API that integrates deeply\" sounds significant change, expensive/difficult. Maybe 2 = significant change or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. Could be 3 = some changes reasonably feasible. Hmm.\n\nCustom middleware across all high-risk AI projects is non-trivial. Might be significant change. I'd rate 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms? Logging, immutable ledger. Acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Probably 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab way unchanged\n\nProposal is automated provenance layer to ensure compliance without manual burden. That's introducing new tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? Might be 2. Not changing relationship between R&D and compliance, just automating. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated logging framework with immutable ledger is fairly common idea. Maybe interesting but common. I'd say 2? Or 1? Provenance logging is common. Custom middleware maybe somewhat uncommon. Probably 2 = interesting. Might be 3? Hmm.\n\nReference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give. Automated logging framework is expected. Could be common mundane boring => 1.\n\nLet's pick originality 2? The proposal mentions immutable ledger which is slightly imaginative. Might be 2.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nReturn JSON? Format unspecified. Probably output lines.\n\nProvide id and then subscale_evidence and subscale.\n\nProbably as:\n\nP2791\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure order.\n\nLet's produce final answer.\n\n",
