        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rate proposal P2481.\n\nProposal: Automated Traceability Framework... internal software suite designed to automatically generate detailed logs and technical documentation mandated by Article 12. Ensures digital twin... middleware captures critical system events, input data matches, precise timestamps... integrate into CI/CD pipeline... minimizes manual compliance overhead while ensuring strictly meet legal mandates for traceability without slowing down R&D.\n\nRate subscales.\n\neffectiveness evidence: one sentence then score.\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal automates logs and technical documentation mandated by Article 12. That directly solves logging mandate. Will solve stated problem without regard for workability? Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability => 4. Or maybe 3? It contributes? It seems will solve main problem of meeting logging mandate. Could be 4.\n\nEvidence: The framework automatically generates Article 12 logs and technical documentation, directly providing the traceability required by Recital 71 and Article 12.\n\nScore 4.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nIt does X: build middleware to get Y: compliance logging. Directly related. So 4.\n\nEvidence: The proposal targets the lab's need to meet Article 12 record-keeping for high-risk systems, which is the identified problem.\n\nScore 4.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nProposal is automated logging middleware integrated into CI/CD. Common? Many labs build compliance tooling. Is it unusual interesting shows some imagination? Could be common mundane boring =1. Or interesting =2. Automated traceability framework is fairly standard compliance engineering. Probably common, mundane. Might be 1 or 2.\n\nRarity reference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expect building internal logging tools. That's common. So originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Automated logging middleware for regulatory traceability is a common compliance engineering response expected from R&D labs.\n\nScore 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal integrates into CI/CD, minimizes manual overhead, ensures meet legal mandates without slowing down R&D. It still treats compliance as logging practice, introduces new tool but doesn't change relationship? It maybe slightly stretches by weaving into CI/CD. That is introducing new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice => 2.\n\nEvidence: The framework adds a middleware/logging tool but keeps compliance as separate record-keeping rather than transforming R&D or its relationship to regulation.\n\nScore 2.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 some changes, or