g. I think it's common. Software library for logging is common. So originality 1.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nThis is standard compliance logging practice; introduces tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools. Might be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 1 paradigm preserving. The proposal says standardized software library... That's standard compliance. I'd say 1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. But they centralize? Hmm.\n\nThe definition for 2: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. That fits. It introduces modular API library. So slightly stretching. Probably 2.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost non-radical changes\n3 = some changes or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change expensive difficult but not impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDeveloping standardized software library across all high-risk AI projects is significant change, requires engineering effort. Not trivial. Maybe 2? Or 3 = some changes reasonably feasible. I'd say 2 = significant change or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies violate no norms\nThis is compliant, acceptable. Score 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence first then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating... Provide lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2710\n\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProvide six pairs in order.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal builds automatic event recording for high-risk AI projects, directly matching Article 12’s logging mandate.\napplicability:4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Capturing timestamps, input matches and user IDs via an API would satisfy the minimum logging items for Annex III systems and general traceability required by Article 12.\neffectiveness:4\n\noriginality_evidence: A standardized logging library for regulatory compliance is a common, mundane industry practice.\noriginality:1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: It introduces a shared tool but still treats compliance as separate logging practice without changing how R&D is organized.\nparadigm_relatedness:2? Might be 1. Let's choose 2 as slightly stretching.\n\nimplementability_evidence: Developing and rolling out a modular API and immutable ledger across all high-risk projects requires significant engineering effort and coordination.\nimplementability:2\n\nacceptability_evidence: Centralized compliance logging is legally required and socially unobjectionable within EU AI regulation.\nacceptability:4\n\nMake sure order: applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nProbably output as JSON with fields.\n\nLet's produce clean output.",
