gs. I'd say contributes to solution, partial. So 3.\n\nEvidence: Automated checks ensure logging meets Article 12 specifications, but the suite itself does not create the logging capability required by the regulation.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nCI/CD testing framework programmatically verifies logging functions. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Could be moderate. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Building test cases around simulated triggers and log assertions requires engineering effort but fits standard CI/CD practices.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Automated testing for compliance is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Automated compliance testing is a common engineering practice and does not conflict with legal or business norms.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nDefinition: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal introduces CI/CD validation suite, introduces new tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So slightly paradigm stretching: 2.\n\nEvidence: The suite adds a new testing tool for compliance yet keeps compliance as a separate logging-practice check rather than redefining R&D-compliance relationship.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\nProposal: automated regulatory validation suite. Unusual, interesting; shows some imagination? Might be common. CI/CD for compliance is fairly common. Probably 2 = interesting. Or 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. I'd say 2 = interesting (not unusual). Let's pick 2.\n\nEvidence: Automated compliance testing for logging is a recognizable engineering approach rather than a rare or surprising invention.\n\nReturn JSON? Instructions: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence and <subscale>. Probably output list.\n\nLet's produce structured output.\n\n",
