l, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nCompliance training and checklists are common mundane. So 1.\n\nWorkability implementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nWorkshop series + mandatory checklist + quarterly audits. Some changes but reasonably feasible. Probably 3? Could be 4 easy? Need workshop development, compliance officer time. Might be some changes. I'll say 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nTraining and checklist is common, acceptable. 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably list:\n\nP1160\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The workshop and milestone templates directly address integrating Article 12 record-keeping documentation into R&D planning to meet traceability requirements. -> score 3? Wait if direct, maybe 4. Hmm.\n\nArticle 12 is about logs automatic recording. Proposal mentions technical documentation template, not logs. So maybe it's implied problem of compliance documentation. I'd keep 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Training will improve documentation timeliness but does not create the automatic event logging capabilities mandated by Article 12(1)-(3). Score 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces checklists and templates but still treats compliance as a separate documentation practice added to R&D rather than redefining the research-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\noriginality evidence: Mandatory compliance training and milestone checklists are a common, mundane