        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal: Open Standard for High-Risk AI Event Logging. Collaborative project to define industry-wide schema for recording high-risk AI events... partner with other EU research centers to publish peer-reviewed specification for interoperable traceability logs. Host initial draft on public repository. Leading definition elevates institutional prestige and ensures scientific interoperability.\n\nWe need rate six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm. 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged.\n\nProposal is open standard for logging, collaborative. Does it change relationship between R&D and compliance? It creates standard, elevates prestige, ensures scientific interoperability. Seems introduces new elements tools/formats/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (schema) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Not really changing relationship to R&D? It partners with others, publishes spec. Could be paradigm stretching? \"changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\". New partners born from compliance - yes. Might be 3. Let's think: It defines industry-wide schema. That's beyond just internal logging. That changes relationship? It creates new offering? Leading definition elevates prestige. Hmm.\n\nProbably paradigm_relatedness = 2? Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. The proposal is about creating standard for logging, not changing what lab researches. I think 2.\n\noriginality: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination 2 = interesting 1 = common mundane boring. Open standard for AI logging? Might be unusual interesting. Not extremely rare? Could be 3. Maybe 2 = interesting. Let's pick 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Collaborative standard is not super novel but somewhat imaginative.\n\nacceptability: 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. 2 = offends... 1 = radically violates... Creating open standard, public repo, seems acceptable. Common? Maybe somewhat uncommon. 3? Could be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Publishing standards is common academic practice. I'd say 4.\n\nimplementability: 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult... 1 = totally infeasible... Collaborative project with partners, peer-reviewed spec, hosting repo. Requires resources, coordination. Not easy low cost. Some changes, reasonably feasible? Might be 3. Could be 2 significant change expensive/difficult. Partnering with other EU research centers is effortful but feasible. I'd say 3.\n\neffectiveness: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, would solve main problem). 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial). 2 = unreasonable unlikely... 1 = solves unrelated problem.\n\nProblem: lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nDoes open standard solve meeting logging mandate? It creates schema but doesn't implement logging in lab's own systems. It helps interoperability but may not directly meet Article 12 requirement for their own high-risk AI systems. It contributes partially to compliance landscape but not solve their immediate logging mandate. So effectiveness = 3? Contribute to solution. Or maybe 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work). Because defining standard doesn't automatically give them logs.\n\nThe stated problem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Open standard does not directly implement logs for their systems, but could make implementation easier later. Might be partial. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial).\n\napplicability: 4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). 3 = solves implied problem that is related... 2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n\nDoes it clearly apply? It's about traceability logs. That's directly related to logging mandate. Even if not immediate implementation, it's about logging. So applicability maybe 4? \"solves an identified problem that is directly related to the stated problem\". The identified problem is meet logging mandate. Creating standard is related but not solving their own logging. Might be 3 = solves implied problem related: ensures scientific interoperability, elevates prestige - implied benefit.\n\nI think applicability = 3. Because it addresses traceability generally, which is related but not directly meeting mandate for their lab.\n\nLet's decide final scores:\nparadigm_relatedness 2\noriginality 3\nacceptability 4\nimplementability 3\neffectiveness 3\napplicability 3\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale in order give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Just list.\n\nI'll produce lines.\n\n",
