        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument...\n\nWe need rate proposal P1210.\n\nText: High-Risk Project Compliance Audit. Comprehensive technical review... identify critical gaps in record-keeping capabilities. Senior engineers will map existing data flows against Article 12 requirements and document every missing telemetry point for each project. Identifying deficits now prevents expensive disruptive retrospective redesigns during final certification phase.\n\nSubscales order: acceptability, implementability, applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nAcceptability evidence: one sentence. Then score.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms...\nProposal is audit mapping gaps. That's common compliance strategy. No violation. So 4.\n\nImplementability: degree easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nAudit requires senior engineers time, mapping data flows. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Probably requires senior engineers. Could be 3.\n\nMaybe 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Yes.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related...\nProblem: must decide how to respond: meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources... Audit maps gaps in record-keeping capabilities against Article12. Directly applies. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\nAudit identifies deficits but does not itself implement logging. It helps plan but doesn't meet mandate. Will contribute to solution, partial. So 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (it helps, but only partial solution). Might be 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree idea preserves/modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nAudit is standard compliance practice. Paradigm preserving? It introduces mapping, but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 1 or 2. Slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Mapping data flows is standard. Probably paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. I'd say 1.\n\nOriginality: degree rare and ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nAudit is common mundane. So 1.\n\nProvide output with id and each subscale evidence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"..._evidence\" first and then \"...\". Could be bullet list.\n\nProbably output like:\nid: P1210\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nDo that.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nAcceptability evidence: The audit is a routine compliance review that aligns with legal requirements and does not offend business norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Mapping data flows requires senior engineering time and documentation but uses existing staff and processes without new technology. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The audit directly addresses the mandate to meet Article 12 logging requirements by identifying missing telemetry points in R&D pipelines. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Identifying gaps now informs later logging implementation but does not itself create the required automatic logs or traceability. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal treats compliance as a separate gap-analysis exercise and leaves the lab’s R&D workflow and research priorities unchanged. Score 1.\n\nOriginality evidence: Comprehensive pre-certification compliance audits are a common, mundane industry practice for regulated AI development. Score 1.\n\nReturn.",
