ogging mandate? It ensures persistence/integrity but does not guarantee automatic recording of events, or recording of specific required data points like period of use, reference database, input data match, identification of persons. It supports storage but not generation. Might be partial.\n\nProbably effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, only partial. Because it ensures storage integrity but doesn't create logging capabilities themselves. The mandate is technically allow automatic recording of events over lifetime. Storage is necessary but not sufficient.\n\nThe proposal says ensures technical viability of traceability claims during external audits without burdening project-level storage. It addresses persistence, not capture. Might be partial solution.\n\nI think effectiveness 3.\n\nEvidence: Immutable storage helps preserve logs for audits but does not by itself create the required automatic event recording or capture mandated fields.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab way unchanged\n\nImmutable compliance data lake is a centralized storage architecture. That's somewhat standard compliance practice. Introduces new element? Centralized write-once store maybe slightly paradigm stretching? It still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nEvidence: The proposal adds a centralized WORM data lake tool for compliance records but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice rather than changing R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nScore 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nImmutable compliance data lake with WORM for AI logs is fairly common. Cloud object store with WORM is standard. Probably common, mundane. So 1.\n\nEvidence: Centralized immutable object storage with WORM policies for regulatory logs is a common, widely used compliance architecture.\n\nScore 1.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nCloud-native object store with strict access controls and WORM is reasonably feasible. Some changes. Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: Deploying a cloud object store with WORM and access controls requires integration work but is commercially available and reasonably feasible.\n\nScore 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal is standard compliance, acceptable. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Immutable logging storage complies with EU AI Act traceability expectations and does not violate legal or business norms.\n\nReturn rating echo id exactly.\n\nNeed output format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? Instructions say return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer). Could be lines.\n\nWe'll produce JSON with fields.\n\nProbably:\n\nid: P2173\napplicability_evidence: \"...\"\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nLet's do that.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nMake sure sentence e