te and/or adapt R&D. Does this solve identified problem directly related? Logging mandate is logs of events. Technical documentation is separate but recital mentions it. Applicability maybe 3 = solves an implied problem that is related (technical documentation vs logging). Hmm proposal mentions fulfills Recital 71 requirement for comprehensible information on system development. Article 12 is record-keeping logs. Might be somewhat off? It says safeguards reputation demonstrating transparency. Could be applicability 3.\n\nAlternative: It directly addresses record keeping via logs and documentation pipeline. Could be 4 solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). Logging mandate includes logs. Documentation pipeline helps traceability. Might be 3.\n\nLet's pick 3 = solves an implied problem related.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\nReasonable and will contribute to solution but partial? Living documentation fulfills Recital 71 but Article 12 requires automatic recording of events/logs over lifetime, logging capabilities enable recording events relevant for risk etc. Documentation pipeline may help but does not fully guarantee automatic event logging per Article 12(2)(a-c). Might be partial. So effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial).\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship...\nProposal introduces dynamic repository linking versions to data etc using MLflow/DVC. That's new tools but still treats compliance as logging practice? It says researchers required to update logs via automated scripts during training cycles. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious.\nLiving Technical Documentation System with MLflow/DVC is common, mundane. Originality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nProvide output.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid P2295\nimplementability_evidence: ... \nimplementability: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nImplementability evidence: Using MLflow or DVC for version-controlled documentation pipelines is a reasonably feasible engineering change requiring integration with training cycles but not radical. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Automated technical documentation and transparency for regulators aligns with legal norms and market expectations for AI compliance. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: The system targets Recital 71 technical documentation and audit trails, which is related to but distinct from Article 12’s mandatory automatic event logging requirement. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: It will improve traceability and compliance documentation and thus contribute to meeting the mandate, though it does not by itself guarantee the specific event-logging capabilities required by Article 12(2). Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces new tools and processes for documentation but keeps compliance as a logging/documentation practice without changing the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Version-controlled model documentation with MLflow/DVC is a common, mundane industry practice for AI labs. Score 1.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
