ntability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nComprehensive review of budgets and compute allocations, quantitative analysis, rebalance funding from low-priority pilots. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Level 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Maybe some changes. Could be 4 easy? Audit is relatively easy. Might be 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\nRebalancing funding from low-priority pilots to mandatory infrastructure. Common strategies that violate no norms. Level 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nAudit budget is standard compliance practice? It's about resource planning. Introduces new elements? Tools, formats, processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces budget audit process but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged? Actually rebalancing funding changes way of working somewhat. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is essentially cost accounting for compliance. That's standard compliance practice. I'd say paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged? It does change funding allocation but not relationship fundamentally. Probably 1 = paradigm preserving.\n\nAlternative: slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice -> 2.\n\nThe audit is a new element (quantitative analysis of storage overhead). Still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nLet's choose 2? Need decide. Paradigm relatedness anchored to this problem. The lab's R&D and compliance obligations relationship unchanged; just reallocate budget. That's not changing relationship, just funding. I'd say paradigm preserving =1.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but also ingenious...\nCommon, mundane, boring? Resource audit for compliance cost is common. Level 1 = common, mundane, boring. Or maybe interesting? It's mundane. So 1.\n\nProvide output with id and subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nNeed format: Return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON-like.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1393\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The audit directly addresses the mandate to provision resources for lifetime logging required by Article 12 and Recital 71. => applicability 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: Reallocating funds ensures budget exists for storage infrastructure but does not itself create logging capabilities or technical compliance. => effectiveness 3\n\nimplementability_evidence: Conducting a budget and compute review and shifting money from low-priority pilots is a reasonably feasible administrative measure. => implementability 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: Rebalancing internal R&D funding toward mandatory compliance costs is a common, non-controversial business practice. => acceptability 4\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a budget analysis tool while keeping compliance treated as a separate logging overhead rather than changing how