nable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve the main problem). Does SDK guarantee? Probably yes if fully implemented. But description is generic.\n\nMaybe 4. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal says eliminates need for researchers to manually implement logging logic, maintaining prototyping velocity while guaranteeing technical compliance. That claim suggests full solution.\n\nI think effectiveness 4.\n\nLet's decide: 4.\n\nNow produce output echoing id exactly. Format? Probably JSON with fields.\n\nReturn rating of this proposal, echoing its id exactly. For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\".\n\nProbably output as lines or JSON. Safer to output JSON with keys.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1939\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\n...\n\nDo in order.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\noriginality_evidence: \"The proposal describes an internal Python SDK that wraps inference calls for automatic logging, which is a common and mundane engineering response to regulatory record-keeping mandates.\" Score 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: \"It introduces a reusable logging tool but keeps compliance as separate logging practice rather than changing how R&D relates to compliance obligations.\" Score 2\n\nimplementability_evidence: \"Building a shared library with hooks for model inference is reasonably feasible with some engineering changes across projects.\" Score 3\n\nacceptability_evidence: \"A standardized internal SDK for immutable event logs is a common, norm-conforming strategy that violates no legal or social sensibilities.\" Score 4\n\napplicability_evidence: \"The SDK directly addresses the mandate to technically allow automatic event recording for high-risk AI systems over their lifetime.\" Score 4\n\neffectiveness_evidence: \"If implemented as described, automatic capture of timestamps, input references and system state would satisfy Article 12 l